        <h2 id="why-switch">1. Why Teachers, Creators &amp; Teams Look Beyond One Wheel</h2>
        <p>
          <em>Wheel of Names</em> earned its reputation honestly: it replaced clunky Flash widgets with a fast, friendly HTML5 spinner that any teacher could pull up in five seconds. For pasting twenty names and calling on one student, it still holds up nicely.
        </p>
        <p>
          The itch to look for an alternative usually starts once a spinner becomes part of a weekly routine—whether that is Monday standups, Friday geography bees, Twitch viewer challenges, or PTA raffles. Four recurring snags push people to compare options:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Starting from a blank box every single time:</strong> Typing out <a href="/bingo-number-generator-1-75">Bingo 1–75</a>, <a href="/us-state-picker-wheel">all 50 US states</a>, <a href="/country-picker-wheel">all 195 UN countries</a>, or <a href="/random-nfl-team-generator">all 32 NFL franchises</a> manually before class or game night burns ten minutes nobody has.</li>
          <li><strong>Messy weighted raffles:</strong> On tools without direct weight controls, giving a VIP ticket buyer 5x odds means pasting their name five separate times—cluttering the wheel until text becomes microscopic.</li>
          <li><strong>Mobile &amp; Chromebook screen clutter:</strong> On a 1366×768 school Chromebook or a narrow phone viewport, heavy floating video ads and shifting headers can push the Spin button half off-screen.</li>
          <li><strong>Verifiable proof for live draws:</strong> Fantasy commissioners and charity organizers increasingly want downloadable CSV logs showing exact timestamps alongside clear documentation of Web Cryptography randomness (<code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="criteria">2. Six Practical Criteria We Used to Test All 10 Spinners</h2>
        <p>
          Rather than skimming feature lists, we evaluated each spinner around real-world friction points teachers, livestreamers, and event hosts actually run into:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Time to first spin:</strong> Can you open the page, paste 30 names from a spreadsheet, and spin in under 15 seconds without creating an account?</li>
          <li><strong>True per-slice weighting:</strong> Can you raise one entry's odds cleanly without duplicating rows?</li>
          <li><strong>Pre-built reference lists:</strong> Does the platform maintain accurate, ready-to-use lists (states, countries, numbers, sports leagues, party prompts), or strictly blank/user-submitted boards?</li>
          <li><strong>Roster privacy &amp; local storage:</strong> Do pasted student names stay strictly inside browser memory, or do you have to sync a cloud account or publish a public Google Sheet?</li>
          <li><strong>Projector, OBS &amp; classroom embed quality:</strong> Is there a clean fullscreen or ad-free iframe mode that looks professional on a whiteboard or Twitch scene?</li>
          <li><strong>Auditability:</strong> Can you export a clean spin history afterward for raffles, grades, or classroom probability charts?</li>
        </ol>

