        <h2 id="reviews">4. Detailed Breakdown of All 10 Alternatives</h2>

        <h3>#1. SpinTheWheelsOnline (Best All-Around Speed, Privacy &amp; Curated Library)</h3>
        <p>
          Built to keep the HTML5 canvas front and center without login walls, <a href="/wheel-of-names">SpinTheWheelsOnline's Wheel of Names</a> and <a href="/picker-wheel">Picker Wheel</a> handle up to 500 entries locally in browser memory. Every outcome pulls entropy from <code>window.crypto.getRandomValues()</code> with rejection sampling so slices avoid modulo skew, and pasted student or giveaway names never get transmitted to a database.
        </p>
        <p>
          The biggest time-saver is its library of <strong>69 curated wheels</strong> grouped into eight hubs—covering <a href="/yes-no-wheel">Yes or No Wheel</a>, <a href="/decision-wheel">Decision Wheel</a>, <a href="/country-picker-wheel">all 195 Countries</a>, <a href="/bingo-number-generator-1-75">Bingo 1–75</a>, <a href="/fantasy-draft-order-wheel">Fantasy Draft Order</a>, <a href="/icebreaker-question-wheel">Icebreaker Questions</a>, and <a href="/presentation-order-wheel">Presentation Order</a>. Built-in controls include <code>1x–20x</code> weight buttons, no-repeat winner removal, live frequency stats, CSV logs, QR codes, and ad-free <code>?embed=1</code> iframes. <em>Main limitation:</em> slices are text and emoji based—if you need uploaded PNG portraits inside wedges, pick Wheel of Names below.
        </p>

        <h3>#2. Wheel of Names (Best for Image Uploads Inside Slices)</h3>
        <p>
          <em>Wheel of Names</em> remains unmatched when you want actual student avatars, product thumbnails, or meme pictures rendered right on the spinning wedges, or direct OAuth import from Google Drive/Sheets. Its sound customization and huge community keep it deservedly popular. The trade-off is that niche reference lists (like all 50 states or 75-ball Bingo) rely on manual setup or searching user galleries rather than verified built-in tools.
        </p>

        <h3>#3. Picker Wheel (Best for Multi-Spin Accumulation Mode)</h3>
        <p>
          <em>Picker Wheel</em> brings clever mechanics you won't find on basic spinners—especially Accumulation Mode, which tallies repeat wins across multiple rounds to crown a cumulative champion (great for classroom point battles or "first to 3 wins" draws). It also offers dedicated letter, state, and team tools, though the density of surrounding display ads can feel cramped on 11-inch school Chromebooks.
        </p>

        <h3>#4. SpinTheWheel.io &amp; SpinTheWheel.app (Best for Simultaneous Multi-Wheels)</h3>
        <p>
          If your gaming video or party night requires spinning <em>three or four wheels at the exact same second</em> (Character + Weapon + Map + Handicap on one screen), <em>SpinTheWheel.io</em> handles synchronized multi-wheel dashboards smoothly and hosts thousands of fandom templates. The downside is a noisier interface and uneven accuracy across user-submitted lists (always double-check community rosters before streaming).
        </p>

        <h3>#5. Wheel Decide (Best Classic Minimalist Blog Embeds)</h3>
        <p>
          One of the web's longest-running spinners, <em>Wheel Decide</em> strips setup down to bare essentials and embeds neatly on older WordPress blogs and school web pages. Its canvas graphics look noticeably dated next to modern high-DPI wheels and it skips CSV exports or frequency tables, yet its simplicity still works well for quick dinner or chore ties.
        </p>

        <h3>#6. AhaSlides Spinner Wheel (Best for Live Audience Presentations)</h3>
        <p>
          Running a conference keynote or corporate all-hands where 150 attendees scan a slide QR code to type their own names straight onto a live PowerPoint wheel? <em>AhaSlides</em> handles crowd-sourced slide decks smoothly. For quick daily cold-calling or solo list spins, however, account setup and session codes add extra overhead.
        </p>

        <h3>#7. AppSorteos Spin the Wheel (Best for Social Media Comment Scraping)</h3>
        <p>
          Social media managers picking winners from posts with 3,000+ Instagram, TikTok, or YouTube comments rely on <em>AppSorteos</em> to ingest post comments automatically, filter duplicate tags, and spin finalists on screen. For draws under 500 entries where you paste a cleaned list directly, <a href="/raffle-wheel">our Raffle Wheel</a> avoids paid tiers and gives cleaner 1x–20x ticket weighting.
        </p>

        <h3>#8. HeySpinner (Best for Shared Live Rooms)</h3>
        <p>
          <em>HeySpinner</em> adds live multiplayer spin rooms with built-in chat so remote friends can watch each spin inside a shared browser link without screen-sharing. If your group already hangs out on Discord, Zoom, or Teams, sharing a single fullscreen browser tab is usually cleaner and avoids moderating a second chat box.
        </p>

        <h3>#9. Flippity Random Name Picker (Best Google Sheets Teacher Ecosystem)</h3>
        <p>
          Teachers living inside Google Classroom love <em>Flippity</em> because a single published Google Sheet powers not only a spinning wheel, but also instant seating charts, random group splits, and tournament brackets. Remember the catch: publishing a Google Sheet to the web requires careful district privacy approval (never publish full student last names!), whereas pasting first names locally into <a href="/classroom-name-picker">Classroom Name Picker</a> stays strictly on your laptop.
        </p>

        <h3>#10. ClassTools &amp; Wordwall (Best Gamified K-12 Review Packs)</h3>
        <p>
          <em>ClassTools.net</em> delivers fun retro arcade energy with its Fruit Machine picker, while <em>Wordwall</em> lets teachers enter one vocabulary list and flip instantaneously between a Random Wheel, Matching Pairs, and Quiz Show. Watch out for Wordwall's free-tier cap on custom resources—once you hit the limit, a zero-paywall wheel is much easier for daily use.
        </p>

        <h2 id="not-for-you">5. Four Honest Cases Where Another Tool Fits Better Than Ours</h2>
        <p>
          No single spinner fits every workflow. Skip SpinTheWheelsOnline and grab one of the tools below if your session specifically calls for:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Uploaded photos on every slice:</strong> Use <em>Wheel of Names</em>—it renders images inside each wedge natively.</li>
          <li><strong>3+ wheels spinning simultaneously on one canvas:</strong> Use <em>SpinTheWheel.io</em> (or open two SpinTheWheelsOnline browser windows side-by-side on desktop if two wheels are enough).</li>
          <li><strong>Live 200-person audience self-entry from phones:</strong> Use <em>AhaSlides</em> so attendees submit their own entries inside your presentation slide.</li>
          <li><strong>Automated scraping of 1,000+ Instagram or TikTok comments:</strong> Use <em>AppSorteos</em> (our wheel intentionally caps lists at 500 entries for browser-local speed and slice clarity).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="privacy-check">6. School IT &amp; Privacy Checklist (FERPA / GDPR Basics)</h2>
        <p>
          Many US school districts and European schools restrict teachers from typing student names into random cloud websites without a signed Data Privacy Agreement (DPA). Before putting any student roster into an online wheel, ask three quick questions:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Does it require a login or save lists to a remote server?</strong> Browser-local tools (like <a href="/classroom-name-picker">Classroom Name Picker</a>) store rosters only in your own device's <code>localStorage</code> and never transmit entries to a backend database.</li>
          <li><strong>Should you use first names + last initials?</strong> Even on local-only tools, pasting <code>Maya R.</code> and <code>Devon K.</code> instead of full legal names is smart classroom hygiene—especially if you ever share your screen on a recorded video lesson.</li>
          <li><strong>Are you sharing a <code>?w=</code> URL publicly?</strong> Remember that shareable wheel links encode your list inside the URL itself so others can open it—never post a share URL containing student names onto a public website or social feed!</li>
        </ol>

